<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Edward, Duke of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1884">1884</a>–<a href="/years/1954">1954</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Edward, Duke of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a>, was a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>, born Duke of Albany in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, who inherited the German duchy in 1900. He fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, was stripped of his British titles and abdicated in November 1918. He later became a prominent supporter of the Nazi regime.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-edward-duke-of-saxe-coburg-and-gotha.jpg" alt="Charles Edward, Duke of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha" width="320" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-2007-0184,_Karl-Eduard_von_Sachsen-Coburg_und_Gotha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles Edward was born in Surrey, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, on 19 July 1884, several months after his father Prince Leopold, Duke of Albany, the youngest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>, died after slipping and hitting his head. His mother was Princess Helen of Waldeck and Pyrmont. He succeeded to his father's titles at birth and grew up as a British prince, a sickly child, and was educated privately, including at Eton College. In 1899 he was selected to succeed his uncle Duke Alfred, because he was young enough to be re-educated as a German, and at fifteen he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, where his cousin Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> took control of his education.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Charles Edward inherited the duchy at sixteen, on Alfred's death in July 1900, and reigned through a regency under Prince Ernst of Hohenlohe-Langenburg until he came of age on 19 July 1905. He married Princess Victoria Adelaide of Schleswig-Holstein-Sonderburg-Glücksburg on 11 October 1905, a bride chosen by Wilhelm, and they had five children. He joined patriotic groups and stressed his loyalty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> festivities and folk costumes, yet he never won much popularity. People in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> thought him absolutist, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> they disliked his foreignness and his English accent.</p><p>At the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to receive an honorary degree from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>. He told his sister that he wanted to fight for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> but felt obliged to return to his duchy, where he did on 9 July, and he sided with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>. Britain removed his titles under the Titles Deprivation Act of 1917, effective 28 March 1919, and he was deposed like the other German princes, ending his rule on 14 November 1918.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the 1920s he described himself as a monarchist but supported violent far-right paramilitary groups. From 1929 he gave financial support to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a>, attracted by its militarism and anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, and he joined it in March 1933 and became an Obergruppenführer in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sturmabteilung">Sturmabteilung</a>. On 1 December 1933 he became head of the German Red Cross, where control passed largely to his deputy Paul Hocheisen in 1934. He served the regime as an informal diplomat, touring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1934.</p><p>American authorities interned him from 1945 to 1946, and a denazification court gave him a minor conviction. He died of cancer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> on 6 March 1954. His sister Alice minimised his role in her memoirs, but the historian Karina Urbach argued that the book is misleading and selective.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles Edward, Duke of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Victoria's son Alfred and grandson <b>Charles Edward</b> later succeeded as dukes.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-coburg-and-gotha">Duchy of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke <b>Charles Edward of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha</b> abdicated in November 1918.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-coburg">Free State of Coburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Claremont, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.36,-0.37&amp;year=1929" aria-label="Claremont on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Charles Edward, Duke of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4980 5736h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4980 5736h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.36,-0.37&amp;year=1929">What was here in 1929? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
